WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.293
Iterator validity depends heavily on the container and the operation being performed.

2
00:00:04.293 --> 00:00:07.513
A std::vector or std::string stores elements contiguously.

3
00:00:07.513 --> 00:00:13.237
When it grows and needs more capacity, it may reallocate and move elements to new storage.

4
00:00:13.237 --> 00:00:18.604
Iterators, references, and pointers that referred to the old storage may no longer be valid.

5
00:00:18.604 --> 00:00:21.466
Erase operations are another common source of defects.

6
00:00:21.466 --> 00:00:24.328
Removing an element ends that element's lifetime.

7
00:00:24.328 --> 00:00:30.410
Depending on the container, iterators at the erased element, and sometimes iterators after it, may be invalidated.

8
00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:36.134
Clearing a container invalidates access to its contained elements because those elements are no longer present.

9
00:00:36.134 --> 00:00:38.280
Other containers have different stability properties.

10
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.289
Lists are designed so many insert and erase operations preserve iterators to other elements.

11
00:00:43.289 --> 00:00:47.224
Associative containers such as maps and sets have their own rules.

12
00:00:47.224 --> 00:00:50.802
Deques have rules that differ from both vectors and lists.

13
00:00:50.802 --> 00:00:54.737
Custom containers and third-party ranges may add their own contracts.

14
00:00:54.737 --> 00:00:59.030
The lesson is not to pretend that one invalidation rule applies everywhere.

15
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:06.186
The defensive habit is to check the specific container contract before storing iterators, reusing iterators after mutation, or passing iterators

16
00:01:06.186 --> 00:01:08.690
across functions that may change the container.

17
00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:11.552
Code review should call out those assumptions explicitly.

18
00:01:11.552 --> 00:01:15.487
A practical pattern is to keep iterator lifetimes short around mutation.

19
00:01:15.487 --> 00:01:24.431
If code needs a stable identity, a key, index with validation, or owning handle may communicate the intent more clearly than a long-lived iterator.

20
00:01:24.431 --> 00:01:29.440
That decision should be made with the container's actual stability rules in mind.
